## 2026-11-22 (rates, -30 r3) — a past read refuses a place with two different answers and gives one answer for the same place twice; round-3 findings settled by standing rule

- **`premise_places_as_of` refuses (21000) when, as known at the instant, one place (its kind and code) has more than one row, relation or distance; the same place recorded twice alike is answered once.** *Why:* a void and a re-record by overlapping transactions leave both known at an instant between them; r3 returned a district twice, or both within and outside (Codex B1, executed), and refused a county recorded again as "two places" (Fable S1). A read that can be ambiguous refuses, one that is not answers. *Not* Codex's refusal of every repeat: where the records agree the answer is certain, and a refusal would make that bill's instant unreadable for good. → -30 r4, `v5.4.2-30-r4-plan.md` B1.
- **An as-known read is settled for an instant only once every transaction begun by then has finished; the places a bill used are the ones it records, not a replay.** Known limit L7 and the read's comment restated (Opus S1, executed); no schema change. True of every as-of read since v5.4.1. The board's stamps item is widened: `clock_timestamp()` does not close it. → -30 r4 header L7; board, Unassigned.
- **A race test's failure says why** (wrong result after a wait, wrong result without one, a broken session, or run in order), and only a wrong result counts as a mutation's catch (Codex S1). → `levy-30.sh`, `mutations-30.sh`.

--     force over the whole of the row's dates (its successive rows may cover
--     them together: a levy keyed by code survives the place's succession).
--     -16's convention: a successor row of the same kind, state and code is
--     the same place. A place is closed only while its levies stay covered
--     (section 2b), so a levy never outlives its place, and a code reused
--     later inherits no levy.
--   * The rate carries at most four decimals (K-06); 0 to 100.
--   * computed_on names only known tax kinds, never the levy's own.
--   * The citation has a letter or a digit.
--   * A utility writes and reads its own levies only.
--   * Every lookup requires every argument (no default, NULL raises).
--
-- KNOWN LIMITS
--   L1. The Comptroller's quarterly files are staged and approved in the
--       application: a proposed rate is not a schema row; the approval is the
--       insert of the utility's levy, which records its approver
--       (created_by). tax_levy_reference_gaps() reports a published change
--       nobody approved.
--   L2. The late-penalty guard, like -07's taxability guard, reads the other
--       table's committed rows: a version and a surcharge rule written at
--       once in two sessions can each pass. Platform setup writes rate
--       items; this is -07's known shape, unchanged.
--   L3. No place below the state is loaded with a sales-tax rate yet: the
--       cities, transit authorities and districts are a later data patch
--       (board: -32), with the published rates as reference facts.
--   L4. The old customer-level exempt columns stay (read by no tax path); their
--       retirement needs compliance_statistics rebuilt under -11's checks
--       and is its own item.
--   L5. The bill line naming its levy and place, the no-tax-on-tax check on
--       a line's bases, an exempt $0.00 line keeping its would-be base, and
--       the defaults on one-off charges and bill lines are the money
--       stream's (board note).
--   L6. An instant before a premise's state was recorded cannot be read
--       (refused). A bill from before go-live is rebilled from its own
--       legacy record, with a supervisor's approval (Kyle 2026-11-05,
--       batch 2 answer 11).
--   L7. "As known at an instant" means "by every transaction that began by
--       it". Every stamp is the writer's transaction start (now()): the
--       template's created_at and closed_at, and those of places and
--       memberships (-16). So a read at an instant is stable only once every
--       transaction that began by then has finished. Until then, and for
--       that instant for good:
--       (a) a replay can differ, silently, from a live read made then: it
--           adds a row whose writer was still open at the instant, or drops
--           one whose close or void began before the row was recorded;
--       (b) a read can refuse ("2 rows" of a levy; two places of one
--           exclusive group, r3; more than one answer for one place, r4)
--           where a successor's writer began before its predecessor's close
--           or void.
--       Every as-of read since v5.4.1 has (a). clock_timestamp() in the
--       template would narrow (b), not close (a) (board, unassigned). The
--       lasting remedy is the core's: a bill records the levies and places
--       it used (L5), and that record, not a replay, is what the bill saw;
--       or a bill is computed at an instant every writer begun by then has
--       finished. The premise's state history is outside (b): its recorder
--       refuses the overlapping writer (r3).
--
-- Preconditions (a real database): no rate item version yet (each would need
-- its penalty-base answer decided, not defaulted); no exemption without
-- service types, or naming one that is not a service; no state agency without
-- a basis; no sales_tax_rate fact while the kind is still a fraction. Each
-- refuses and counts the rows. On a re-apply each is skipped once its change
-- is in place.
-- ============================================================================

